/** CRS key-points review: approved batch 1, four syllabus-ordered lessons. */
window.CRS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"creation-covenants","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"CREATION, HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY, THE FALL AND GOD'S COVENANTS — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe opening chapters of Genesis answer important questions in the biblical account: Who created the world? Why do human beings have dignity? Why did people disobey God? How did God continue to relate to humanity? Read each story in its own setting rather than mixing up events.\n\n1. THE CREATION ACCOUNTS (Genesis 1–2)\nCreation means God's bringing the world into existence. Genesis 1 describes a structured sequence:\nDay 1 — light and the separation of day from night.\nDay 2 — the expanse separating waters.\nDay 3 — dry land, seas and vegetation.\nDay 4 — sun, moon and stars.\nDay 5 — creatures of the sea and birds.\nDay 6 — land animals and human beings.\nDay 7 — God rested, blessing and making the seventh day holy.\nGenesis 2 focuses on the human setting: the man, the garden of Eden, the command concerning the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, the animals and the formation of the woman. These chapters have different emphases; do not treat the order of every detail as a single identical list.\n\n2. HUMAN DIGNITY AND RESPONSIBILITY (Genesis 1:26–28; 2:15–24)\nBeing made in God's image gives human beings a special standing and responsibility; it does not mean that people are gods. Humans are instructed to be fruitful, exercise stewardship over creation and care for the garden. Genesis 2 describes the woman as a suitable partner, and the union of husband and wife. JAMB may ask about the responsibilities rather than only the creation day.\n\n3. DISOBEDIENCE AND ITS CONSEQUENCES (Genesis 3)\nThe serpent questioned God's command. The woman and man ate from the prohibited tree. They became ashamed, hid and shifted blame. The narrative describes consequences involving pain, difficult work, broken relationships and expulsion from Eden. The serpent is cursed; the ground is cursed because of the man. Distinguish what the text says about each person.\n\n4. CAIN, ABEL AND THE FLOOD (Genesis 4; 6–9)\nCain and Abel offered gifts to God. Cain killed Abel after becoming angry and jealous; God questioned Cain and judged him, yet placed a protective mark on him. In Noah's time, widespread wickedness led to the flood. Noah obeyed God's instructions to build the ark. After the flood, God made a covenant that a flood would not again destroy all flesh. The rainbow is its sign.\n\n5. ABRAHAM AND THE COVENANT (Genesis 12; 15; 17; 22)\nGod called Abram to leave his homeland, promising land, descendants and blessing to the nations. His trust was tested repeatedly. Circumcision was the sign of the covenant in Genesis 17. Abraham's willingness to offer Isaac in Genesis 22 illustrates obedience and faith; God provided a ram instead. Isaac is the covenant heir, while Ishmael also receives a promise of descendants.\n\n6. COMPARE THE COVENANTS\nNoah — promise concerning the earth and living creatures; sign: rainbow (Genesis 9).\nAbraham — descendants, land and blessing; sign: circumcision (Genesis 17).\nIsrael at Sinai — covenant relationship with commands and obligations (Exodus 19–20).\nDo not swap the signs or assume all three covenants were made with the same people.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): On which day were humans created in Genesis 1?\nSolution: Genesis 1:26–31 places humans on the sixth day. The seventh day is God's rest. Answer: Sixth day.\n\nExample (ii): What was the sign of God's covenant with Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 9:12–17 identifies the rainbow. Circumcision belongs to the Abrahamic covenant. Answer: Rainbow.\n\nExample (iii): What does God's image in human beings imply?\nSolution: The passage gives humanity dignity and responsibility for creation. It does not claim that humans have divine powers. Answer: Dignity and responsible stewardship.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the sixth and seventh days should not be confused. Compare Noah's and Abraham's covenant signs. Describe two consequences of disobedience in Genesis 3.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button below to attempt mapped JAMB questions, then explain why the distractors are wrong."},"old":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"Creation means God's bringing everything into existence. Genesis 1 presents an ordered creation, including land animals and human beings on the sixth day and God's rest on the seventh. Human beings are made in God's image: they reflect and represent him and receive responsibility for the earth. This is not a claim that people are gods or that God has the same physical shape as a person. It supports respect for every person's dignity, including people of a different tribe, social position or ability.\n\nGenesis 2 concentrates on the garden and human relationships. The river divides into Pishon, Gihon, Tigris (Hiddekel) and Euphrates. After the statement that the man needs a suitable helper, the narrative describes the animals being brought to him; the formation of the woman follows. The account ends with husband and wife becoming one flesh. Eve's name is linked with being the mother of all living. Do not confuse this with the earlier words “bone of my bones.”\n\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving commitments. God's covenant with Noah includes living creatures and the promise that a flood will not again destroy all flesh. Its sign is the rainbow. God's promises to Abraham include descendants, land and blessing reaching other nations. Circumcision is its sign. Abraham's obedience in leaving his familiar home is a major example of faith in Hebrews 11. God's promise concerning Ishmael recognises him as Abraham's offspring, while Isaac carries the particular covenant line.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Noah: rainbow; Abraham: circumcision; Sinai: law and covenant obedience.\n• The Sabbath ends the first creation sequence; marriage closes the second account.\n• Being made in God's image is likeness and responsibility, not physical appearance or human divinity.\n\nGenesis places the firmament, separating waters, on the second day and the sun, moon and stars on the fourth. Genesis 9 connects the prohibition of bloodshed with God's image in humanity. Noah obeyed God's instructions and entered the ark with his household under God's covenant. Genesis 11 names Terah, Abram, Sarai and Lot as leaving Ur; Haran was their stopping place. At Moriah Abraham named the place “The LORD will provide.” At Bethel Jacob promised a tenth of what God gave him. Keep these places, persons and covenant responses distinct.\n\nGenesis 6:3 names one hundred and twenty years; interpretations differ over whether this is a lifespan limit or a period before judgement. Genesis 1 calls the gathered waters seas and the dry land earth.\n\nGenesis 2 assigns garden cultivation and care. Genesis 3 explicitly curses the serpent and ground; the humans receive painful consequences and expulsion. Noah's ark floats as the waters rise.\n\nGenesis calls Noah's material gopher wood; the precise tree identification remains uncertain."}},{"id":"moses-exodus","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"MOSES, THE EXODUS AND GOD'S COVENANT WITH ISRAEL — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe Exodus tells how the Israelites were delivered from slavery in Egypt and formed into a covenant community. Follow the events in order: oppression, Moses' call, confrontation with Pharaoh, Passover, crossing the sea, wilderness and Sinai.\n\n1. MOSES' EARLY LIFE AND CALL (Exodus 1–4)\nPharaoh ordered the killing of Hebrew male infants. Moses' mother hid him, then placed him in a basket on the Nile; Pharaoh's daughter found him. After Moses fled Egypt to Midian, God called him from the burning bush at Horeb. God revealed the divine name and commissioned him to lead Israel. Moses objected that the people might not believe him and that he was not an effective speaker. God gave signs and appointed Aaron to speak with him.\n\n2. PHARAOH AND THE PLAGUES (Exodus 5–12)\nPharaoh resisted Moses' demand to let the Israelites go. The plagues demonstrated God's power and challenged Pharaoh's refusal. Remember the final plague: the death of the firstborn. The Passover instructions involved a lamb, blood on the doorposts and a meal eaten in readiness to depart. The blood marked Israelite households in the narrative. Passover commemorates deliverance; it is not the same event as the crossing of the sea.\n\n3. CROSSING THE SEA AND WILDERNESS PROVISION (Exodus 13–17)\nGod guided the people by a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night. At the sea, Moses stretched out his hand and the waters divided. After escaping Egypt, the Israelites faced shortages and complained. God provided manna and quail, and water in the wilderness. These stories test whether students can distinguish a crisis, the people's response and God's provision.\n\n4. THE SINAI COVENANT (Exodus 19–20; 24)\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving promises and responsibilities. At Sinai, God called Israel to be a treasured people and a holy nation if they kept the covenant. The Ten Commandments describe duties towards God and other people. Moses received the law; the people were called to obey. The Sabbath command concerns keeping a day holy, not the sign of the Noahic covenant.\n\n5. THE GOLDEN CALF (Exodus 32–34)\nWhile Moses was on the mountain, Aaron made a golden calf at the people's request. They worshipped it, violating the covenant. Moses interceded for them, and the narrative includes judgment and renewal. The central lesson is that covenant membership does not remove the obligation to obey God.\n\n6. LEADERSHIP, SPIES AND ENTRY INTO CANAAN (Numbers 13–14; Deuteronomy 34; Joshua 1–6)\nTwelve spies explored Canaan. Joshua and Caleb urged trust, while the others spread fear. The generation's refusal had consequences during the wilderness years. Moses saw the promised land but did not lead the people into it. Joshua succeeded Moses, led the crossing of the Jordan and directed the conquest of Jericho. Distinguish Moses' crossing of the sea from Joshua's crossing of the Jordan.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why was Aaron assigned to assist Moses?\nSolution: Moses objected that he was not an effective speaker (Exodus 4:10–16). God appointed Aaron as his spokesman. Answer: To speak on Moses' behalf.\n\nExample (ii): Which event immediately preceded Israel's departure from Egypt?\nSolution: The final plague was the death of the firstborn, and the Passover instructions accompanied it (Exodus 12). Answer: The final plague and Passover.\n\nExample (iii): Who succeeded Moses as Israel's leader?\nSolution: Deuteronomy 34 and Joshua 1 identify Joshua. Aaron was Moses' brother and priest, not his successor as national leader. Answer: Joshua.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the purpose of Passover. List two responsibilities under the Sinai covenant. Explain why the crossing of the Red Sea and the crossing of the Jordan must not be confused.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to test the sequence of events and their biblical references."},"old":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"The Exodus is the account of God's deliverance of Israel from slavery in Egypt. At Moses' call, God gave signs because Moses feared the Israelites would not believe he had been sent. When the rod first became a snake, Moses fled; he took it by the tail only after God's instruction. At the sea, the Egyptians acknowledged that the LORD fought for Israel when their chariots were impeded. God avoided the shorter Philistine route lest seeing war make the people return to Egypt.\n\nIn the wilderness, food and water tested trust and obedience. Israel's complaints about food were heard, and God promised meat in the evening and bread in the morning. The people were to gather manna as commanded, rather than store extra in distrust. The special instructions before the Sabbath also required obedience. The Sabbath command concerns keeping the seventh day holy; its numerical position differs between Christian traditions, although the biblical command remains the same. Common Protestant lists number it fourth; Catholic and Lutheran lists usually number it third.\n\nAt Sinai, the people were consecrated and washed their garments. They were not told to touch the mountain: boundaries protected the holy meeting. In Exodus 24 they promised to do God's words; Moses recorded them, arranged sacrifices and confirmed the covenant with blood. After the golden calf, Moses interceded by appealing to God's promises to the patriarchs.\n\nKeep the two rock incidents distinct. Exodus 17 records a command to strike the rock at Horeb. Numbers 20 records a command to speak to the rock in the wilderness of Zin; Moses struck it instead. A familiar action can therefore be wrong in a different setting.\n\nBefore entry into Canaan, the spies assessed the land and its inhabitants.\n\nCommon trap: identify the particular episode and sequence before choosing a familiar name or action.\n\nThe Hebrew midwives spared boys because they feared God. Moses encountered God in a bush that burned without being consumed. Exodus 4 gives three signs: rod becoming a snake, diseased hand restored, and river water becoming blood; these are not the numbered plagues. Exodus 24 names oxen as the peace offerings. After the golden calf came a plague. On the Sabbath, disobedient gatherers found no manna; overnight hoarding on ordinary days produced worms. Numbers 20 excludes Moses from leading Israel into the land. \n\nMoses acts as mediator between God and the people. Pharaoh seeks his life after the Egyptian's killing, so he flees. The first manna comes in the wilderness of Sin, distinct from Zin. Shiphrah and Puah receive families because they fear God.\n\nA cloud guides by day and fire by night. Acts 7 connects Moses' defence of his Hebrew kin with attempted deliverance, without endorsing violence."}},{"id":"samuel-saul-david","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"SAMUEL, SAUL AND DAVID — LEADERSHIP, FAITH AND OBEDIENCE\n\nINTRODUCTION\nIsrael moved from the period of judges to kingship. This lesson explains why the people requested a king, how Saul became king, why his leadership failed and how David rose to prominence. JAMB often tests names, actions and consequences.\n\n1. SAMUEL'S CHILDHOOD AND CALL (1 Samuel 1–3)\nHannah prayed for a child and promised to dedicate him to God. Samuel served under Eli at Shiloh. When God called Samuel at night, he initially thought Eli was speaking. Eli taught him how to respond. Samuel's call shows attentiveness and obedience. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas; Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah. Do not mix them up.\n\n2. ISRAEL REQUESTS A KING (1 Samuel 8)\nThe elders complained that Samuel's sons acted dishonestly and requested a king like the surrounding nations. Samuel warned them about the demands a king would make, including military service, labour and taxation. The issue was not simply that kingship itself was always forbidden; the account also stresses Israel's rejection of God's kingship.\n\n3. SAUL'S APPOINTMENT AND EARLY LEADERSHIP (1 Samuel 9–11)\nSaul, son of Kish from Benjamin, was anointed by Samuel and publicly chosen. He gained support after delivering Jabesh-gilead from the Ammonites. Distinguish private anointing from public selection.\n\n4. SAUL'S DISOBEDIENCE (1 Samuel 13; 15)\nSaul offered sacrifice when Samuel had not arrived as expected. Later he failed to carry out the command concerning Amalek fully, sparing Agag and valuable livestock. Samuel taught that obedience is better than sacrifice. Saul's downfall is associated with disobedience, not merely his military weakness.\n\n5. DAVID'S ANOINTING AND GOLIATH (1 Samuel 16–17)\nGod sent Samuel to Jesse's family at Bethlehem. David, the youngest son, was chosen. The lesson stresses that God looks at the heart, not outward appearance. David confronted Goliath with a sling and stones, trusting God rather than relying on Saul's armour.\n\n6. DAVID, JONATHAN AND SAUL (1 Samuel 18–31)\nJonathan and David developed a close covenant friendship. Saul became jealous of David's popularity and repeatedly sought to kill him. David spared Saul when he had opportunities to harm him. This demonstrates restraint and respect for the king's position.\n\n7. DAVID AS KING: ACHIEVEMENTS AND FAILURES (2 Samuel 5–12)\nDavid united the tribes and made Jerusalem his capital. He brought the ark to Jerusalem. He also committed adultery with Bathsheba and arranged Uriah's death. Nathan confronted him with a parable; David acknowledged his sin and faced consequences. A leader's achievements do not excuse wrongdoing.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why did Israel's elders request a king?\nSolution: Samuel's sons did not follow his example and accepted bribes; the elders wanted a king to judge and lead them like other nations (1 Samuel 8). Answer: Corrupt judges and the desire for a king like other nations.\n\nExample (ii): What did Samuel mean by obedience being better than sacrifice?\nSolution: Saul attempted to justify keeping livestock after disobeying God's instruction (1 Samuel 15). Religious offerings could not replace obedience. Answer: God's command must be obeyed rather than excused by ritual.\n\nExample (iii): Who confronted David over Bathsheba and Uriah?\nSolution: The prophet Nathan used a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's lamb (2 Samuel 12). Answer: Nathan.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nName Eli's sons and Samuel's sons separately. Give two reasons Saul lost divine approval. Explain how David responded to Nathan's rebuke.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise names, events, causes and consequences."},"old":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"Samuel served God under Eli. Hannah had dedicated him in fulfilment of her vow; Eli later prayed that she would have more children. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas. Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah, who became judges but took bribes. When Samuel reported judgement on Eli's household, Eli acknowledged the LORD's authority to do what seemed good to him.\n\nIsrael's demand for a king marked a change from the earlier leadership associated with judges. Theocracy means rule under God's authority; it does not mean Israel had no human leaders. Saul's rescue of Jabesh-gilead brought public support. He refused revenge on earlier opponents because the LORD had brought deliverance. In the Amalekite campaign he spared what he was commanded to destroy and afterwards set up a monument to himself. The Kenites were warned to leave because they had shown Israel kindness.\n\nDavid first came to Saul's court to serve as a musician; fighting Goliath belongs later. Saul's promised reward for defeating Goliath included his daughter in marriage. Seven of Jesse's sons passed before Samuel before David was brought from tending sheep. David's hiding places include Adullam and Hachilah; the Ziphites reported his whereabouts to Saul.\n\nDavid refused Abishai's proposal to kill Saul. He would not incur guilt by striking the LORD's anointed and trusted God to determine Saul's end. At Ziklag, he sought God's direction before pursuing the Amalekites. His later kingship faced Absalom's revolt, which forced him to flee Jerusalem.\n\nSamuel's sons differ from Eli's; David's first summons concerns music. Trust never excuses dishonesty, pride or abuse.\n\nAn unnamed man of God warned Eli before Samuel received his message. Saul later sought a medium when threatened by the Philistines. David's spies confirmed Saul's arrival at Ziph. Israel pursued the Philistines after Goliath's fall. At Ziklag David consulted God through the priest and ephod. His servants feared he might harm himself after his child's death. David's repentance after confronting his own wrongdoing contrasts with Saul's self-justification. During Absalom's revolt a soldier respected David's order to spare his son; the victorious troops entered quietly because David mourned. The story does not excuse David's abuse of Uriah.\n\nAt Ashdod, the Philistines suffered tumours after bringing in the ark; possessing a sacred object did not guarantee blessing.\n\nSaul says he feared the people and obeyed their voice. Before consulting the medium, he receives no answer through dreams, Urim or prophets. Their wrongdoing, not merely Samuel's age, explains the complaint.\n\nEli warns his sons about sinning against God, but fails to restrain them. God announces judgement to Samuel over that failure. Saul's servant suggests consulting a seer about the lost donkeys. Amalek's attack after the Exodus explains the later command."}},{"id":"kings-division","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"SOLOMON, THE DIVIDED KINGDOM AND RELIGIOUS REFORMS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nAfter David, Solomon's reign brought great achievements but also problems. His successor Rehoboam faced a divided kingdom. Later kings and prophets challenged idolatry and injustice. Students should distinguish Israel (north) from Judah (south).\n\n1. SOLOMON'S WISDOM (1 Kings 3–4)\nAt Gibeon, Solomon asked God for wisdom to govern the people rather than for wealth or long life. The judgment between two women claiming one child illustrated his discernment. His wisdom became widely known, including to the queen of Sheba.\n\n2. THE TEMPLE (1 Kings 5–8)\nSolomon built the Temple in Jerusalem, with help from Hiram of Tyre, who supplied timber and craftsmen. The Temple became a centre of worship. At its dedication, Solomon prayed that God would hear the prayers of his people. David had prepared for the project, but Solomon built it.\n\n3. SOLOMON'S FAILURES (1 Kings 11)\nSolomon's many foreign wives turned his heart towards other gods. The narrative also describes substantial building and labour demands. His wealth and wisdom did not prevent spiritual compromise.\n\n4. WHY THE KINGDOM DIVIDED (1 Kings 12)\nAfter Solomon's death, the people asked Rehoboam to reduce the heavy burden imposed under his father. Older advisers recommended a gentle answer; younger advisers recommended a harsher response. Rehoboam followed the harsh advice. Ten northern tribes followed Jeroboam; Judah remained under Rehoboam, with Benjamin associated with the southern kingdom. The split also reflects earlier tensions and prophetic judgment.\n\n5. NORTH AND SOUTH: DO NOT CONFUSE THEM\nNorthern kingdom: Israel; first king Jeroboam I; important later capital Samaria.\nSouthern kingdom: Judah; first king Rehoboam; capital Jerusalem.\nJeroboam established worship centres with golden calves at Bethel and Dan to discourage travel to Jerusalem. Rehoboam was Solomon's son; Jeroboam was not.\n\n6. PROPHETS AND REFORM (1 Kings 17–19; 21; 2 Kings 22–23)\nElijah challenged Baal worship during Ahab's reign, including the contest at Mount Carmel. He condemned Ahab's injustice in the seizure of Naboth's vineyard. Much later, Josiah of Judah initiated reforms after the Book of the Law was found in the Temple during repairs. He removed idolatrous practices and renewed covenant commitment. Do not attribute Josiah's reform to Solomon or Jeroboam.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What did Solomon request at Gibeon?\nSolution: He asked for an understanding heart to judge God's people (1 Kings 3). Answer: Wisdom to govern.\n\nExample (ii): What immediate decision helped cause the division under Rehoboam?\nSolution: He rejected the elders' advice and threatened heavier burdens (1 Kings 12). Answer: His harsh response to the people's request.\n\nExample (iii): Which king reformed worship after the Book of the Law was found?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22–23 names Josiah. The book was found during Temple repairs. Answer: Josiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nCompare Jeroboam and Rehoboam. Explain why Solomon's reign is remembered for both achievement and failure. Name two actions associated with Josiah's reform.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise the divided kingdom, key kings and religious reforms."},"old":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"Solomon succeeded David and built the Temple; David's friend Hiram of Tyre provided materials. Solomon's organisation also involved heavy labour demands. When the people later asked Rehoboam to lighten their burden, they referred to this harsh service. Burdensome policies differ from religious offences.\n\nSolomon's turning towards other gods brought the announcement that much of the kingdom would be torn away. Division awaits his son's reign, retaining territory for David's sake. Rehoboam rejected the elders' advice to answer the people kindly and adopted the younger men's harsh counsel. First Kings 12:15 explains this as a turn of affairs fulfilling God's earlier word. Solomon's son Rehoboam differs from northern king Jeroboam.\n\nJeroboam established golden calves at Bethel and Dan. In later generations, royal misconduct affected the whole nation. Manasseh's shedding of innocent blood is specifically connected with judgement on Judah in 2 Kings 24. The judgement concerns injustice as well as false worship; religion never excuses violence against innocents.\n\nJosiah responded to the discovery of the law book by tearing his clothes and seeking the LORD. Huldah's reply says that his tender heart, humility and tears were the reason the announced disaster would not come during his lifetime. Do not substitute the general fact of his righteous reign for this particular reason.\n\nHis reforms included defiling Topheth to stop the sacrifice of children to Molech. At Bethel, he demolished the altar and high place and destroyed the idolatrous installation. Descriptions of breaking, burning and reducing to dust belong to the wider action; short multiple-choice options can oversimplify it.\n\nJosiah understood the book before seeking guidance; illiteracy was not the issue. Ceremonies never cancel justice.\n\nAsa removed ancestral idols. During Solomon's lifetime, Hadad became an adversary; the tearing away of the kingdom was assigned to his son's reign. Solomon's attempt to kill Jeroboam followed the threat to royal succession. At the Temple dedication Solomon recalled the promise of Davidic continuity with its condition of faithful obedience. Josiah deposed Judah's idolatrous priests; distinguish this from killing priests at the northern shrines. His reforms included Passover observance and sparing the tomb of the earlier prophet who foretold them. Jehoiachin surrendered when Babylon besieged Jerusalem.\n\nAt Gibeon, after a thousand offerings, God appears in a dream. Solomon's threatened division of a baby reveals maternal compassion. Josiah's workmen need no accounting because they act honestly. Asa burns Maacah's Asherah image at Kidron.\n\nJosiah dies fighting Neco at Megiddo. Solomon receives wisdom from God; at Gibeon, long life is conditional on obedience. Josiah renews the covenant after the book's discovery.\n\nSecond Kings 23 describes an exceptionally complete Passover, not proof that none had occurred since Judges. Ahijah foretells the division during Solomon's reign. Hilkiah finds the law book."}},{"id":"elijah-elisha","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"ELIJAH AND ELISHA — GOD'S POWER, TRUE WORSHIP AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA prophet is someone called to communicate God's message. Elijah and Elisha served in the northern kingdom of Israel, but their ministries were not identical. Elijah is especially remembered for confronting idolatry and injustice; Elisha continued the prophetic ministry through teaching, miracles and intervention in community life.\n\n1. WHY ELIJAH CONFRONTED AHAB (1 Kings 16–18)\nKing Ahab and Queen Jezebel promoted Baal worship. Baal was treated by his worshippers as a god associated with fertility and rain. Elijah's announcement of drought challenged the claim that Baal controlled the rain. At Mount Carmel, the prophets of Baal called without receiving an answer; Elijah prayed and the LORD answered with fire. The people were called to choose whom they would worship.\n\n2. DISTINGUISH THE LOCATIONS\nBrook Cherith — Elijah was fed by ravens during the drought (1 Kings 17).\nZarephath — a widow shared food with Elijah; her flour and oil did not run out, and her son was restored to life.\nMount Carmel — the contest concerning true worship took place (1 Kings 18).\nBrook Kishon — the captured prophets of Baal were taken there (1 Kings 18:40).\nUnder a broom tree — Elijah, fleeing Jezebel, expressed despair (1 Kings 19).\nMount Horeb — Elijah encountered God's presence and received renewed direction (1 Kings 19).\nThese are different events. Do not answer every Elijah location question with “Mount Carmel”.\n\n3. NABOTH'S VINEYARD: JUSTICE AND POWER (1 Kings 21)\nNaboth refused to surrender his inherited vineyard. Jezebel arranged false accusations against him; he was killed, and Ahab took possession. Elijah announced God's judgement. The lesson concerns abuse of authority, false testimony, greed and respect for another person's rights.\n\n4. ELIJAH AND ELISHA (2 Kings 2)\nElisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit. This expression is linked to the inheritance of a firstborn son; it is not simply a request for twice as many miracles. Elijah was taken up in a whirlwind, and Elisha continued the prophetic work. Elisha's ministry includes the healing of Naaman (2 Kings 5), the widow's oil (2 Kings 4), and the healing of Jericho's water (2 Kings 2).\n\n5. HOW TO ANSWER QUESTIONS ON THESE PROPHETS\nIdentify the person, the exact event, and the passage. For example, Naaman's healing is associated with Elisha, while the contest with Baal's prophets is associated with Elijah. Never use the general statement “both performed miracles” to ignore the event named in the question.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where did Elijah challenge the prophets of Baal?\nSolution: Read 1 Kings 18. The contest was held at Mount Carmel. Answer: Mount Carmel.\n\nExample (ii): Why did Elijah condemn Ahab over Naboth's vineyard?\nSolution: Naboth was unjustly killed through a plot so that Ahab could take his inheritance (1 Kings 21). Answer: The abuse of royal power and injustice against Naboth.\n\nExample (iii): Which prophet instructed Naaman to wash in the Jordan?\nSolution: The account is in 2 Kings 5 and concerns Elisha. Answer: Elisha.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the drought challenged Baal worship. Distinguish Mount Carmel from Brook Kishon. Give one example of injustice and one example of Elisha's ministry.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's Practice JAMB Past Questions button to test the events and their biblical settings.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nElijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."},"old":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"Elijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."}},{"id":"prophetic-justice","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"PROPHETS, TRUE WORSHIP AND SOCIAL JUSTICE — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nBiblical prophets were messengers of God. Their work included warning, teaching, correcting rulers and calling people to live faithfully. Social justice means treating people fairly, especially those who are vulnerable to exploitation. In the prophetic books, religious ceremonies without justice and obedience are strongly criticised.\n\n1. AMOS: JUSTICE MUST ACCOMPANY WORSHIP\nAmos preached against exploitation, dishonest trade, corrupt courts and comfortable people who ignored the suffering of the poor. In Amos 5:24, justice and righteousness are compared to flowing water. The image teaches that fairness should be continuous, not an occasional performance.\nAmos 2:6 — the righteous are sold for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals.\nAmos 4:1 — the “cows of Bashan” describe wealthy women in Samaria who oppress the poor.\nAmos 5:11 — people who trample the poor are warned about houses they will not enjoy.\nAmos 8:4–6 — dishonest trading practices and exploitation are condemned.\nAmos 8:11 — a famine of hearing the LORD's words is announced. This is not an ordinary shortage of food.\n\n2. HOSEA: COVENANT LOVE AND FAITHFULNESS\nHosea used marriage imagery to explain Israel's unfaithfulness and God's continuing call to return. Covenant love includes loyalty and mercy. Hosea 6:6 places steadfast love and knowledge of God above empty ritual. It does not mean worship is always wrong; it means ritual cannot replace faithful conduct.\n\n3. ISAIAH AND MICAH: RIGHT LIVING\nIsaiah 1:16–17 calls people to stop doing evil, seek justice, correct oppression and defend the vulnerable. Micah 6:8 summarises what God requires: do justice, love kindness and walk humbly with God. Learn the specific prophet and passage rather than treating every statement about justice as interchangeable.\n\n4. TRUE AND FALSE PROPHECY\nA prophet's message should not be judged merely by how pleasing it sounds. Jeremiah challenged false assurances of peace while wrongdoing continued. Students should distinguish a genuine warning that calls people to obedience from a flattering message that ignores injustice.\n\n5. EXAM TRAPS\nThe “cows of Bashan” are not the cattle offered in sacrifice; the phrase criticises oppressive luxury (Amos 4:1).\nThe famine in Amos 8:11 is a famine of hearing God's word.\nAmos, Hosea, Isaiah and Micah all address wrongdoing, but the wording and setting of each passage matter.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is the famine described in Amos 8:11?\nSolution: The verse identifies a famine of hearing the words of the LORD, not a lack of bread or water. Answer: A shortage of hearing God's word.\n\nExample (ii): What does Micah 6:8 require?\nSolution: The passage names justice, kindness and humble walking with God. Answer: Just conduct, mercy and humility before God.\n\nExample (iii): Who are the “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1?\nSolution: The prophet addresses privileged women of Samaria who oppress the poor. Answer: Wealthy, oppressive women of Samaria.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain social justice in your own words. Name two practices condemned by Amos. Distinguish outward worship from covenant faithfulness.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked JAMB questions to practise identifying each prophet and the correct passage.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nA biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."},"old":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"A biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."}},{"id":"repentance-restoration","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"REPENTANCE, PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY AND RESTORATION — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nRepentance means turning away from wrongdoing and returning to God in thought and action. It is more than feeling sorry. Restoration means rebuilding a damaged relationship or community. The prophets taught that God's judgement is serious, but they also proclaimed opportunities for genuine change.\n\n1. HOSEA: RETURN TO THE LORD (Hosea 14)\nHosea calls Israel to confess sin, stop trusting foreign powers as saviours and abandon idols. His teaching joins honest words to a changed life. Hosea 14:9 explains that the LORD's ways are right: the upright walk in them, while rebels stumble. The difference is the response of the people, not a change in God's ways.\n\n2. JEREMIAH: CHANGE OF HEART AND A NEW COVENANT\nJeremiah criticised empty confidence in the Temple when people's behaviour contradicted their worship (Jeremiah 7). Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a new covenant: God's law written on hearts, knowledge of God and forgiveness. The promise concerns inner transformation, not merely replacing one building or ruler with another.\nJeremiah 3:15 describes shepherds who lead with knowledge and understanding. In this passage “shepherds” refers to leaders, not only people caring for animals.\n\n3. EZEKIEL: PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY (Ezekiel 18)\nEzekiel rejects the idea that an individual must automatically bear guilt for another person's wrongdoing. Each person is responsible for their own conduct. The wicked person who turns away from wrongdoing may live; a righteous person who turns to evil cannot rely on past good behaviour as an excuse.\nThe proverb about fathers eating sour grapes and children's teeth being set on edge expresses the attitude Ezekiel corrects.\n\n4. JONAH: REPENTANCE AT NINEVEH (Jonah 3–4)\nJonah announced judgement against Nineveh. Its people responded with fasting and turning from evil, and God did not bring the threatened destruction. Jonah was displeased by God's mercy. The account teaches both the seriousness of repentance and the breadth of divine compassion.\n\n5. HOW TO DISTINGUISH THE PASSAGES\nHosea 14 — return, confession and rejection of idols.\nJeremiah 31 — the new covenant and inward law.\nEzekiel 18 — individual responsibility and the possibility of change.\nJonah 3 — Nineveh's repentance.\nThese themes overlap, but the passages answer different examination questions.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is central to the new covenant in Jeremiah 31?\nSolution: Jeremiah says God's law will be written on people's hearts and that God will forgive their sins. Answer: Inward obedience and forgiveness.\n\nExample (ii): According to Ezekiel 18, can a wicked person change their outcome?\nSolution: Yes. The chapter says that a wicked person who turns away from evil and does what is right may live. Answer: Yes, through genuine repentance.\n\nExample (iii): Why was Nineveh spared after Jonah's warning?\nSolution: Jonah 3 describes the people's response to the warning and their turning from evil. Answer: They repented.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDefine repentance and restoration. Explain personal responsibility in Ezekiel 18. Compare Jeremiah's new covenant with Hosea's call to return.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nPractise the passages using the linked JAMB questions.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nRepentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."},"old":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"Repentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."}},{"id":"exile-return","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"EXILE, FAITHFULNESS AND THE RETURN TO JERUSALEM — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nExile is the forced removal of people from their homeland. Judah's exile followed the Babylonian conquest of Jerusalem. Biblical writers connect the disaster with persistent disobedience and injustice. The return from exile later involved rebuilding community life and worship.\n\n1. THE FALL OF JERUSALEM (2 Kings 25; Jeremiah 39)\nBabylon captured Jerusalem during the reign of Zedekiah. He fled, was caught in the plains of Jericho, and was taken to Riblah for judgement. His sons were killed before him, and his eyes were put out. Remember the distinction: he was captured near Jericho but judged at Riblah. Jerusalem and its Temple were destroyed, and many inhabitants were deported; some poorer people remained to work the land.\n\n2. JEREMIAH AND EZEKIEL: DIFFERENT CALLS\nJeremiah 1 describes God's call of Jeremiah before his birth, his objection that he was young, and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel 2–3 describes a commission to speak to a rebellious people, whether or not they listen. The prophets both warned God's people, but their calling stories must not be mixed up.\n\n3. DANIEL: FAITHFULNESS IN A FOREIGN LAND\nDaniel and his companions faced pressure to compromise their worship.\nDaniel 3 — Nebuchadnezzar ordered worship of a golden image. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused and survived the fiery furnace.\nDaniel 6 — under Darius, Daniel's habit of praying became the basis of an accusation. He was thrown into a den of lions and survived.\nThe furnace and lions' den involved different rulers, accusations and people.\n\n4. THE RETURN AND REBUILDING\nAfter Babylon fell to Persia, Cyrus permitted exiles to return and rebuild the Temple (Ezra 1). Zerubbabel is associated with the rebuilt Temple; Ezra with teaching the law and reform; Nehemiah with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls and organising the community. The Temple and the city walls are not the same project.\nOpposition and discouragement occurred during rebuilding, but the community was called to perseverance and covenant obedience.\n\n5. WHAT THE EVENTS TEACH\nFaithfulness is possible even under pressure. Courage does not always mean the absence of danger. Rebuilding after disaster requires leadership, cooperation, worship and just conduct.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where was Zedekiah captured, and where was he judged?\nSolution: Jeremiah 39 and 2 Kings 25 distinguish the plains of Jericho from Riblah. Answer: Captured near Jericho; judged at Riblah.\n\nExample (ii): Which king ordered the worship of the golden image in Daniel 3?\nSolution: Daniel 3 identifies Nebuchadnezzar. Answer: Nebuchadnezzar.\n\nExample (iii): Who is especially associated with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls?\nSolution: Nehemiah led the wall-building project described in the book bearing his name. Answer: Nehemiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDistinguish Daniel 3 from Daniel 6. Explain the difference between Temple rebuilding and wall rebuilding. State one lesson from the exile.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked past questions to practise people, places and the sequence of events.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nExile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."},"old":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"Exile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."}}];
